<GuidePage title='First-Render JavaScript - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 7 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>First-Render JavaScript</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    The known isn't only content; it's also the code required to show it. Every kilobyte of
    JavaScript the first render demands is a toll paid before the page becomes interactive. This
    chapter is about keeping that toll to what the first paint actually uses: boundaries first, <code
      >dynamic()</code
    > second.
  </p>

  <h2 id='tax'>7.1 What first-render JavaScript costs</h2>
  <p>
    Server rendering gets content on screen before any JavaScript runs (<a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a
    >), but the page isn't <em>done</em> at first paint. The client bundle still has to download, parse,
    and execute before hydration attaches your event handlers, and until then the page is a photograph:
    visible, correct, and deaf. Everything in the bundle gates that moment, whether or not it participates
    in it. The dialog no one has opened, the chart on a collapsed tab, the editor behind an "Edit" button:
    if they're in the main bundle, the user pays for them before the first button click works.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the cost isn't limited to interactivity. <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> can't start prefetching until
    it hydrates (<a href='04-ssr.html#dynamic-loading'>§4.5</a>), so a heavy bundle doesn't just
    delay this page, it delays the head start on the
    <em>next</em> one. Note what doesn't help here: the React Compiler removes re-render busywork, not
    bytes. The only cure for shipping too much code is shipping less of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What determines the bundle's size is exactly one thing:
    <strong>the module graphs of your client components</strong> (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#what-it-means'>§5.1</a
    >). Every <code>"use client"</code> file, everything it imports, and everything
    <em>those</em> import: that is the bundle. Server components contribute zero bytes, no matter how
    heavy their dependencies are; their markdown parsers and date libraries run on the server and stay
    there (<a href='04-ssr.html#benefits'>§4.2</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id='boundary-first'>7.2 The first lever is the boundary, not the loader</h2>
  <p>
    Because the bundle is the sum of client module graphs, the biggest reductions come from
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a>, not from this chapter. A page that moves its
    boundary from the layout to three leaf buttons hasn't "optimized its bundle"; it has deleted
    most of it. <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    Get the order of operations right:
    <strong
      >first ask "why is this client at all?", and only then ask "should this client code load
      later?"</strong
    >
    Lazy-loading a God component (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#god-components'>§5.9</a
    >) is treating a symptom: the page now downloads its architectural mistake slightly later. This
    chapter's tool, <code>dynamic()</code>, is for the code that has legitimately earned its place
    on the client but hasn't earned a seat in the
    <em>first-render</em> bundle.
  </p>

