  <h2 id='dynamic'>7.3 <code>dynamic()</code>: loading client code on demand</h2>
  <p>
    <code>next/dynamic</code> is <code>React.lazy()</code> plus Suspense in one call: it turns an import
    into a separate chunk, gives you a <code>loading</code> fallback for the gap, and, critically,
    <strong>moves the download to the moment the component first renders</strong>. Combined with
    conditional rendering, "first renders" becomes "the user actually asked for it":
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/AnalyticsSection.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* A charting library is parsing and drawing machinery the first paint never
   uses; the section starts collapsed. The named export comes back from the
   import() promise; the loading fallback mirrors the chart's geometry so
   nothing shifts when it lands (§10). */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> EnrollmentChart = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(
  () =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./EnrollmentChart"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.EnrollmentChart),
  { <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>: () =&gt; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ChartSkeleton</span> /&gt; }
);

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">AnalyticsSection</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [expanded, setExpanded] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Collapsible</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">expanded</span>={expanded}
      <span class="tok-prop">onToggle</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setExpanded</span>((prev) =&gt; !prev)}
    &gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Mounted only when expanded; the chunk downloads on first open. */}</span>
      {expanded &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">EnrollmentChart</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Collapsible</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    This is the same shape as
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>'s dynamically-imported
    dialog: encapsulation and lazy loading are natural partners, because the component that owns the
    interaction is also the only one that knows when the code is actually needed. Two mechanics to
    get right:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Call <code>dynamic()</code> inside client components.</strong>
      <Chip k='next' />
      <Chip k='critical' /> When a <em>server</em> component dynamically imports a client component, the
      code is not split, and
      <code>ssr: false</code> there is a build error. Do the splitting in the client file that renders
      the thing; a server component's own imports never needed splitting in the first place (they ship
      nothing).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>ssr: false</code> is for code that can't run on the server at all</strong>:
      libraries that touch <code>window</code> at import time, canvas and map widgets. It skips the server
      prerender pass entirely, so reach for it only when the component genuinely can't produce server
      HTML; otherwise you're giving away first-paint content for nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Declare lazy components at module top level, never inside a component.</strong>
      <Chip k='react' />
      <Chip k='critical' /> The React docs call this out directly: a <code>lazy()</code>/<code
        >dynamic()</code
      > created inside a component body is a <em>new</em> component on every render, so the loaded component
      unmounts and loses all state each time the parent re-renders.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/campus/CampusMapPanel.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* The map library reads window at import time - it cannot render on the
   server, period. ssr: false skips its server pass; the skeleton reserves
   the map's exact dimensions so the late arrival displaces nothing (§10.3). */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> CampusMap = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(
  () =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./CampusMap"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.CampusMap),
  { <span class="tok-prop">ssr</span>: <span class="tok-kw">false</span>, <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>: () =&gt; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">MapSkeleton</span> /&gt; }
);</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Libraries can be split without splitting a component at all - a plain
    <code>await import()</code> inside the handler that needs it. A fuzzy-search library used only after
    the user types, a CSV exporter used only on the export click:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/table/ExportButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ExportButton</span>({ rows }: { rows: <span class="tok-fn">Row</span>[] }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">loading</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* The CSV library enters the browser on the first export click -
             not one page load sooner. */</span>
          <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { toCsv } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"@/lib/csv"</span>);
          <span class="tok-fn">download</span>(<span class="tok-fn">toCsv</span>(rows));
        })
      }
    &gt;
      Export
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='candidates'>7.4 What earns a dynamic import, and what doesn't</h2>
  <p>
    The test is one question: <strong>can the user see or use it at first paint?</strong> If not, its
    code shouldn't be in the first-render bundle.
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Candidate</th>
        <th>Verdict</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Dialogs, drawers, popovers, command palettes</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Always dynamic</strong>: invisible until an interaction, by definition (<a
            href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a
          >).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Heavy libraries: charts, rich-text editors, PDF/file viewers, syntax highlighters,
          spreadsheet grids
        </td>
        <td>
          <strong>Always dynamic</strong>: these dwarf your own code; none belong in a main bundle.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Panels behind collapsed sections or non-default tabs</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Dynamic</strong> when the content is client code; if the panel is server-rendered, prefer
          route segments (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#tabs'>§9.3</a>) and there's
          nothing to split.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Handler-only libraries: exporters, parsers, fuzzy search</td>
        <td>
          <strong><code>await import()</code> in the handler</strong>: no component wrapper needed.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Small interactive leaves visible at first paint (buttons, inputs, menus)</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Leave them alone</strong>: splitting a 2&nbsp;kB component adds a round trip and a
          loading flash to save nothing. The chunk has to be worth its own request.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Anything above the fold that the first paint shows</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Never dynamic with a fallback the user will see</strong>: you'd be manufacturing a
          skeleton for content the server could have delivered finished.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Don't guess: <strong>measure</strong>. <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
    <code>@next/bundle-analyzer</code> shows exactly which imports own the bytes; the browser's coverage
    panel shows how much of the bundle the first paint actually executed. The two usual convictions: a
    heavy library imported by a component that's visible for 5% of sessions, and a "shared utils" module
    that drags a whale in through one re-export.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          The bundle is the client module graphs:
          <strong>shrink it with boundaries first</strong> (<a
            href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a
          >), <code>dynamic()</code> second.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Anything invisible at first paint loads behind <code>dynamic()</code></strong>:
          dialogs, drawers, collapsed panels, non-default tabs.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Heavy libraries never ride the main bundle</strong>: charts, editors, viewers
          split at their leaf; handler-only libraries via
          <code>await import()</code>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>dynamic()</code> calls live <strong>inside client components</strong>; server-side
          dynamic imports of client components don't split, and
          <code>ssr: false</code> there is an error.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Don't split the small or the visible</strong>: a chunk must be worth its own
          network round trip.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='imp' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

